            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Equating squared distances gives ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "y=3x",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "; substitute ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "y=x^2",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " and factorise.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $P(x,y)$ lie on $y=x^2$. Since $AP=BP$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{(x-2)^2+(y-1)^2}&=\\sqrt{(x+1)^2+(y-2)^2}\\\\(x-2)^2+(y-1)^2&=(x+1)^2+(y-2)^2\\\\x^2-4x+4+y^2-2y+1&=x^2+2x+1+y^2-4y+4\\\\2y-6x&=0\\\\y&=3x.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstitute $y=x^2$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&=3x\\\\x^2-3x&=0\\\\x(x-3)&=0\\\\x&=0,\\ 3.\\end{align*}$$\n\nUsing $y=x^2$, the corresponding $y$-coordinates are $0$ and $9$. Therefore, $P$ is $(0,0)$ or $(3,9)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
